(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-anatomy-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Anatomy Exam 4 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"In anatomical position, which of the two leg bones is the medial, weight-bearing bone?","choices":["The tibia","The fibula","The patella","The talus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tibia is the larger medial bone of the leg and bears essentially all the body's weight transmitted to the ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which carpal bone lies most medially in the proximal row, adjacent to the ulna?","choices":["Capitate","Trapezium","Scaphoid","Pisiform"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pisiform lies most medially in the proximal carpal row, adjacent to the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Through the greater sciatic notch (foramen) passes which major nerve to the lower limb?","choices":["The suprascapular nerve","The phrenic nerve","The sciatic nerve","The radial nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sciatic nerve exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic notch/foramen to supply much of the lower limb. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which scapular landmark hooks anteriorly and serves as an attachment point for muscles and ligaments, visible from the anterior view?","choices":["Acromion","Infraglenoid tubercle","Coracoid process","Superior angle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The coracoid process is the anteriorly hooking projection of the scapula, visible from the anterior view and serving as a muscle/ligament attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Besides the pelvis, which general feature can the skeleton reveal about an individual, per the material?","choices":["Blood type","Approximate age","Eye color","Native language"],"answer":1,"exp":"The material notes that skeletal features can indicate characteristics such as age, in addition to sex and body size. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"What structures make up an individual coxal bone's acetabular region?","choices":["Ischial spine, ischial tuberosity, and ischial ramus","Iliac crest, iliac fossa, and iliac tuberosity","Pubic crest, pubic tubercle, and pubic symphysis","Acetabular notch, acetabular fossa, and lunate surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"The acetabular region includes the acetabular notch, acetabular fossa, and lunate surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which small pit on the head of the femur anchors the ligament of the head (ligamentum teres)?","choices":["The fovea capitis","The greater trochanter","The intercondylar fossa","The linea aspera"],"answer":0,"exp":"The fovea capitis is the small central pit on the femoral head that anchors the ligament of the head of the femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which structure on the humerus separates the anatomical neck from the shaft, marking the former growth-plate boundary and a common fracture site?","choices":["The deltoid tuberosity","The intertubercular sulcus","The surgical neck","The radial groove"],"answer":2,"exp":"The surgical neck of the humerus lies just distal to the anatomical neck and tubercles, marking the region distal to the (now-fused) growth plate — a common fracture site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which posterior landmark is smaller than the greater sciatic notch and located below the ischial spine?","choices":["Iliac crest","Ischial tuberosity","Posterior inferior iliac spine","Lesser sciatic notch"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lesser sciatic notch is the smaller notch located below the ischial spine, distinct from the greater sciatic notch above it. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What structures does the appendicular skeleton include?","choices":["The limbs, pectoral girdle, and pelvic girdle","The limbs and vertebral column only","The skull, vertebral column, and rib cage","The pectoral girdle and skull only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The appendicular skeleton includes the limbs, the pectoral girdle, and the pelvic girdle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which mnemonic phrase is given in the material for remembering the order of the carpal bones?","choices":["\"Sally left the party to take Cathy home\"","\"Scared lovers try positions that they can't handle\"","\"Some lovers try positions that they cannot handle\"","\"She looks too pretty, try to catch her\""],"answer":0,"exp":"The material gives the mnemonic \"Sally left the party to take Cathy home\" for scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform, trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the arcuate line?","choices":["A ridge on the lateral ilium marking gluteal muscle attachment","The rim encircling the acetabulum on the coxal bone","A ridge on the medial ilium marking part of the pelvic brim","The bony edge bordering the obturator foramen anteriorly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The arcuate line is a ridge on the medial surface of the ilium that forms part of the pelvic brim (the boundary of the pelvic inlet). — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which fossa lies on the anterior (costal) surface of the scapula, facing the ribs?","choices":["The supraspinous fossa","The subscapular fossa","The infraspinous fossa","The glenoid fossa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The subscapular fossa is the broad concavity on the anterior (costal) surface of the scapula that faces the rib cage. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which prominent roughened ridge runs longitudinally along the posterior shaft of the femur?","choices":["The intertrochanteric line","The soleal line","The pectineal line","The linea aspera"],"answer":3,"exp":"The linea aspera is the prominent longitudinal ridge on the posterior surface of the femoral shaft that serves as a major muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The acetabulum serves as the socket for which articulation?","choices":["The knee joint with the patella","The hip joint with the head of the femur","The sacroiliac joint with the sacrum","The shoulder joint with the humerus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The acetabulum is the deep lateral socket of the hip bone that receives the head of the femur to form the hip joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which mandibular process forms the movable part of the temporomandibular joint?","choices":["The coronoid process","The alveolar process","The mental protuberance","The condylar process"],"answer":3,"exp":"The condylar process of the mandibular ramus bears the head that articulates with the temporal bone at the temporomandibular joint. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which feature is unique to cervical vertebrae and transmits the vertebral artery?","choices":["The costal facets","The transverse foramina","The large oval body","The sacral hiatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical vertebrae uniquely possess transverse foramina in their transverse processes, which transmit the vertebral arteries. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Does the hyoid bone articulate directly with any other bone?","choices":["No — it is suspended by ligaments and does not articulate with any bones, making it extremely mobile","Yes — it articulates directly with the styloid process of the adjacent temporal bone","Yes — it articulates directly with the thyroid cartilage of the larynx below it","Yes — it articulates directly with the posterior body of the mandible above it"],"answer":0,"exp":"The hyoid bone does not articulate with any other bones; it is suspended by the stylohyoid ligaments, which makes it extremely mobile. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How many cervical, thoracic, and lumbar vertebrae are there, respectively?","choices":["7 cervical, 5 thoracic, 12 lumbar","5 cervical, 12 thoracic, 7 lumbar","7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar","12 cervical, 7 thoracic, 5 lumbar"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 7 cervical vertebrae, 12 thoracic vertebrae, and 5 lumbar vertebrae. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Why are ribs 11 and 12 called \"floating ribs\"?","choices":["They are the only ribs without a head, neck, and body","They articulate with two thoracic vertebrae instead of one","They lack tubercles","They have no anterior cartilage attachment to the sternum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ribs 11 and 12 are called floating (vertebral) ribs because they have no anterior costal cartilage attaching them to the sternum. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"Which feature is characteristic of all cervical vertebrae?","choices":["Fusion with adjacent vertebrae","A transverse foramen in each transverse process","A costal facet for rib articulation","A large, block-shaped vertebral body with a posteriorly pointed spinous process"],"answer":1,"exp":"All cervical vertebrae have transverse foramina, and their transverse processes are fused to the costal processes. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What passes through the stylomastoid foramen?","choices":["The optic nerve alone, exclusively, with no other named structures","The oculomotor nerve alone, controlling extraocular eye movement","Air conducting sound to the eardrum, the internal carotid artery, and the facial nerve","The spinal cord alone, as it descends from the brainstem"],"answer":2,"exp":"Per the material, structures using the stylomastoid foramen/external acoustic meatus/carotid canal region include air that conducts sound to the eardrum, the internal carotid artery, and the facial nerve. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which posterior part of a typical rib articulates with the bodies of the thoracic vertebrae?","choices":["The head of the rib","The costal cartilage","The costal groove","The anterior shaft"],"answer":0,"exp":"The head of the rib, at its posterior end, articulates with the bodies of the thoracic vertebrae at the costovertebral joints. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What is the sacral promontory?","choices":["An opening for passage of the cauda equina, located at the sacral apex","A fibrous ligament connecting the sacrum directly to the coccyx below it","A structure of the sacrum, part of its listed anatomical landmarks near the base","A synovial joint formed between the sacrum and the adjacent ilium"],"answer":2,"exp":"The sacral promontory is one of the anatomical structures of the sacrum, located near its base. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"The epiphyseal (growth) plate is located in which region of a growing long bone?","choices":["Within the medullary cavity","Between the epiphysis and the diaphysis","At the very center of the diaphysis","Surrounding the outer periosteum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate is the cartilage zone located between the epiphysis (end) and the diaphysis (shaft) of a growing long bone. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which vertical ethmoid ridge projects into the cranial cavity as an attachment for the falx cerebri?","choices":["The sella turcica","The mastoid process","The crista galli","The mental spine"],"answer":2,"exp":"The crista galli is the upward ethmoid ridge that projects into the cranial cavity and anchors the falx cerebri. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Through which sphenoid opening does the optic nerve pass toward the eye?","choices":["The foramen magnum","The jugular foramen","The optic canal","The mastoid foramen"],"answer":2,"exp":"The optic canal in the lesser wing of the sphenoid transmits the optic nerve from the cranial cavity toward the orbit. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which two openings in the sphenoid transmit branches of the trigeminal nerve (mandibular division) and other structures near the base of the skull?","choices":["Foramen magnum and jugular foramen","Cribriform plate foramina","Foramen ovale and foramen spinosum","Optic canal and superior orbital fissure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The foramen ovale and foramen spinosum are openings in the sphenoid, with the foramen ovale transmitting the mandibular nerve (V3). — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which bony projection of the temporal bone lies just behind the ear and can be felt as a hard bump?","choices":["The coronoid process","The mastoid process","The pterygoid process","The odontoid process"],"answer":1,"exp":"The mastoid process is the rounded projection of the temporal bone behind the ear that serves as a muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the epiphyseal plate (growth plate), and where is it located?","choices":["A cavity located within the diaphysis that contains yellow bone marrow","Hyaline cartilage between the diaphysis and epiphysis; the main site of longitudinal growth","Dense compact bone tissue found specifically at both articulating ends of long bones","A fibrous connective tissue covering that surrounds the entire outer surface of the bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The epiphyseal plate (growth plate) is made of hyaline cartilage located between the diaphysis and epiphysis, serving as the main site of longitudinal growth in children and adolescents. — Anatomy of the Axial Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which two muscles are exceptions to the general rule that forearm/hand movers originate on the humerus, because they instead originate on the scapula?","choices":["Long head of triceps brachii and long head of biceps brachii","Pronator teres and the supinator, both forearm rotators","Flexor carpi radialis and flexor carpi ulnaris, both wrist flexors","Brachialis and brachioradialis, both elbow flexors"],"answer":0,"exp":"The long head of triceps brachii (inserting on the olecranon) and the long head of biceps brachii (inserting on the radial tuberosity) are exceptions, originating on the scapula rather than the humerus. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which nerve supplies the quadriceps femoris of the anterior thigh?","choices":["The sciatic nerve","The femoral nerve","The obturator nerve","The superior gluteal nerve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The femoral nerve supplies the anterior thigh muscles, including the quadriceps femoris and sartorius. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"The anterior compartment of the leg, which dorsiflexes the foot, is supplied by which nerve?","choices":["The deep fibular nerve","The tibial nerve","The superficial fibular nerve","The obturator nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"The deep fibular (deep peroneal) nerve supplies the anterior leg compartment that dorsiflexes the foot and extends the toes. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the anterior compartment of the arm?","choices":["Biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis","Triceps brachii, biceps brachii, and brachialis","Biceps brachii, brachioradialis, and supinator","Triceps brachii alone, the posterior compartment muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior compartment of the arm contains the biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"The four rotator cuff muscles are the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and which fourth muscle?","choices":["The subscapularis","The teres major","The deltoid muscle","The coracobrachialis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rotator cuff comprises the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis, which stabilize the shoulder joint. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which muscle produces flexion of the hallux (great toe) specifically, as an extrinsic muscle of the foot?","choices":["Flexor hallucis longus","Extensor hallucis longus","Flexor digitorum longus","Abductor hallucis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Flexor hallucis longus produces flexion of the hallux. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Which muscle flexes the thumb specifically, distinguishing it from the flexor digitorum muscles that act on the other digits?","choices":["Flexor digitorum profundus","Flexor pollicis longus","Extensor pollicis brevis","Flexor digitorum superficialis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexor pollicis longus produces flexion of the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which muscle forms the lateral bulge of the forearm and flexes the elbow with the forearm in a thumb-up position?","choices":["The pronator teres","The triceps brachii","The supinator muscle","The brachioradialis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The brachioradialis forms the lateral forearm bulge and flexes the elbow most effectively with the forearm midway between pronation and supination. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which muscle pronates the forearm and is located deep, spanning between the distal ulna and radius?","choices":["Supinator muscle","Brachioradialis muscle","Pronator quadratus","Pronator teres muscle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pronator quadratus pronates the forearm and is a deep muscle spanning between the distal ulna and radius. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which muscle group makes up the medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh?","choices":["Pectineus, adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus, gracilis, and obturator externus","The hamstrings -- biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus -- of the posterior thigh compartment","Iliopsoas, quadriceps femoris, and sartorius, the muscles of the anterior thigh compartment","Gluteus maximus and the lateral rotator group of the posterior hip region"],"answer":0,"exp":"The medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh contains the pectineus, adductor brevis, adductor longus, adductor magnus, gracilis, and obturator externus. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which three extensor muscles act on the wrist, producing extension and either abduction or adduction?","choices":["Flexor carpi radialis, flexor carpi ulnaris, and palmaris longus, the primary wrist flexor group","Abductor pollicis longus, extensor pollicis brevis, and extensor pollicis longus, acting on the thumb","Extensor digitorum, extensor indicis, and extensor digiti minimi, acting primarily on the fingers","Extensor carpi radialis longus, extensor carpi radialis brevis, and extensor carpi ulnaris"],"answer":3,"exp":"Extensor carpi radialis longus and brevis extend and abduct the wrist, while extensor carpi ulnaris extends and adducts the wrist — together the three named wrist extensors. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which three muscles are collectively called the hamstrings?","choices":["Biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus","Semimembranosus, semitendinosus, and gracilis","Biceps femoris, sartorius, and popliteus","Popliteus, sartorius, and rectus femoris"],"answer":0,"exp":"The biceps femoris, semimembranosus, and semitendinosus are collectively called the hamstrings. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which large superficial back muscle elevates, retracts, and depresses the scapula depending on the fibers used?","choices":["The serratus anterior","The subclavius","The teres major","The trapezius"],"answer":3,"exp":"The trapezius is a broad superficial muscle whose upper, middle, and lower fibers elevate, retract, and depress the scapula respectively. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"The intrinsic muscles forming the fleshy mass at the base of the thumb are collectively called what?","choices":["The hypothenar muscles","The thenar muscles","The lumbrical muscles","The interosseous muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thenar muscles form the thenar eminence, the fleshy mass at the base of the thumb, and move the thumb. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which three muscles make up the hamstring group of the posterior thigh?","choices":["Rectus femoris and the three vasti","Gluteus maximus, medius, minimus","Adductor longus, brevis, magnus","Biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The hamstrings are the biceps femoris, semitendinosus, and semimembranosus of the posterior thigh. — Appendicular Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which extrinsic tongue muscle protrudes (sticks out) the tongue?","choices":["The styloglossus muscle","The hyoglossus muscle","The masseter muscle","The genioglossus"],"answer":3,"exp":"The genioglossus, arising from the mandible, protrudes the tongue and depresses its center. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"The paired levator ani and coccygeus muscles together form what structure?","choices":["The thoracic diaphragm","The rotator cuff","The pelvic diaphragm","The rectus sheath"],"answer":2,"exp":"The levator ani and coccygeus together form the pelvic diaphragm, the muscular floor that supports the pelvic organs. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"How many paired flat and strap muscles form the anterolateral abdominal wall?","choices":["Four paired muscles","Two paired muscles","Six paired muscles","Eight paired muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Four paired muscles form the anterolateral abdominal wall: the rectus abdominis, external oblique, internal oblique, and transversus abdominis. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which muscle group forms the pelvic diaphragm portion of the anal triangle, including the iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus?","choices":["Obturator internus","External anal sphincter","Coccygeus","Levator ani"],"answer":3,"exp":"Levator ani, which includes the iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus, is a key muscle of the anal triangle. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Which vertical, strap-like abdominal muscle flexes the trunk and shows the 'six-pack' segmentation?","choices":["The transversus abdominis","The rectus abdominis","The external oblique","The internal oblique"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rectus abdominis is the vertical strap muscle that flexes the trunk; its tendinous intersections create the six-pack appearance. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"What is the motor end plate, and what receptors does it contain?","choices":["A region of the spinal cord that contains the motor neuron cell bodies themselves","The presynaptic terminal of the motor neuron, containing synaptic vesicles filled with acetylcholine","The connective tissue sheath surrounding and protecting the entire neuromuscular junction","A specialized region of the sarcolemma containing nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChRs)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The motor end plate is a specialized region of the sarcolemma (postsynaptic membrane) that contains nicotinic acetylcholine receptors (nAChRs), which bind ACh and initiate an end-plate potential, and has junctional folds to increase surface area and receptor density. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What connective tissue layer surrounds bundles of muscle fibers, known as fascicles?","choices":["Endomysium","Sarcolemma","Epimysium","Perimysium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The perimysium surrounds bundles of muscle fibers, which are called fascicles. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which erector spinae muscle is the most lateral of the three, running from the iliac crest/sacral crest/lumbar spinous processes to the inferior aspect of ribs 6-12?","choices":["Longissimus thoracis","Iliocostalis lumborum","Multifidus muscle","Spinalis thoracis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Iliocostalis lumborum is the most lateral of the three erector spinae muscles, running from the iliac crest, sacral crest, and lumbar spinous processes to the inferior aspect of ribs 6-12 near their angles, producing extension and rib depression. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"The deep (intrinsic) back muscles function primarily to do what?","choices":["Move the upper limb at the shoulder","Compress the abdominal organs","Extend and stabilize the vertebral column","Rotate the eyeball in the orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep intrinsic back muscles extend, rotate, and stabilize the vertebral column and maintain posture. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The back muscles are broadly divided into which two categories?","choices":["Voluntary and smooth muscles","Cardiac and skeletal muscles","Superficial and deep muscles","Flexor and rotator cuff muscles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The back muscles are divided into superficial extrinsic muscles that move the limb and deep intrinsic muscles that move the spine. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"The right and left rectus abdominis muscles are separated in the midline by which fibrous band?","choices":["The inguinal ligament","The linea alba (white line)","The nuchal ligament","The interosseous membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"The linea alba is the fibrous midline band, formed by the fusing aponeuroses, that separates the two rectus abdominis muscles. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which sphincter-like muscle encircles the eye and closes the eyelids?","choices":["The orbicularis oris","The buccinator muscle","The orbicularis oculi","The zygomaticus major"],"answer":2,"exp":"The orbicularis oculi encircles the eye and closes the eyelids in blinking and squinting. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which broad, thin muscle sheet of the neck tenses the skin and depresses the lower lip?","choices":["The masseter muscle","The temporalis muscle","The buccinator muscle","The platysma muscle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The platysma is a broad, thin sheet in the superficial neck that tenses the skin and pulls down the lower lip and corner of the mouth. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which anterior neck muscle, when acting together bilaterally, flexes the neck, and when acting unilaterally, turns the head and face to the opposite side?","choices":["Thyrohyoid muscle","Sternocleidomastoid","Sternothyroid muscle","Omohyoid muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The sternocleidomastoid, innervated by CN XI (accessory nerve), flexes the neck when both sides act together, and turns the head/face to the opposite side when acting alone on one side. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which deep cheek muscle compresses the cheek against the teeth, aiding in chewing and blowing?","choices":["The buccinator","The masseter muscle","The temporalis muscle","The platysma muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The buccinator forms the muscular wall of the cheek and compresses it against the teeth, keeping food between the molars and aiding blowing. — Axial Musculature - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 14"}]};
